DESIGN CHOICES AND JUSTIFICATIONS (summary; details in the answers below)

1. Pointer checking before use (method 1 in the spec).
   Why: a bad pointer is found before we take any lock or allocate
   anything, so killing the process never leaks resources. It is also
   easier to read and debug than the page-fault method.

2. One shared struct child_process per parent/child pair, with two
   semaphores (load_sema, wait_sema).
   Why: exec must not return before the load result is known, and
   wait must work whether the child exits before or after the parent
   waits. A semaphore remembers an "up", so both orders work.

3. parent_alive flag, updated with interrupts off.
   Why: the shared record must be freed exactly once, by whichever of
   parent or child finishes last, so neither ever touches freed memory.

4. One global filesys_lock around all file system code, including
   load() and process_exit().
   Why: the provided file system is not thread-safe, and the spec says
   process_execute() also touches files. sys_exec() does not hold the
   lock while waiting for the child, because the child's load() needs
   it (holding it would deadlock).

5. Per-process linked list of open files; fd numbers from a counter
   starting at 2.
   Why: no fixed limit on open files (the FAQ asks for none), easy
   cleanup on exit, and a process can only reach its own files.

6. Executable kept open with file_deny_write() until the process exits.
   Why: the spec's "deny writes to executables" feature; closing the
   file in process_exit() re-allows writes automatically.

7. Hard limits on arguments (128 words, one 4 KB stack page), checked
   before pushing.
   Why: the spec allows a reasonable limit; failing the load is safer
   than overflowing the stack page or argv[].

8. pid == tid.
   Why: each process has exactly one thread, so a separate pid table
   would add code without any benefit.

