---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B2: Describe how file descriptors are associated with open files.
>> Are file descriptors unique within the entire OS or just within a
>> single process?

When open() succeeds, we malloc a struct process_file, store the
struct file * from filesys_open(), give it a number from
allocate_fd(), and add it to the current thread's file_name_list.
Every other file system call (read, write, seek, tell, filesize,
close) finds the entry with get_file(fd), which searches only the
current process's list. close() closes the file, removes the entry
and frees it. process_exit() closes and frees any entries still open.

The numbers come from one shared counter, so a number is never handed
out twice anywhere in the OS. But a process can only use the numbers in
its own list: if process A passes one of process B's fds, get_file()
returns NULL and the call fails. So in practice each process has its
own set of file descriptors, as the project requires. Opening the same
file twice gives two different fds with separate file positions,
because filesys_open() returns a new struct file each time.

>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

We check every user pointer before the kernel uses it, using
is_user_vaddr() and pagedir_get_page():

  - check_valid_ptr(p): exits with -1 if p is NULL, at or above
    PHYS_BASE, or not mapped in the process's page directory.
  - check_valid_buf(buf, size): checks the first byte, the start of
    every page the buffer crosses, and the last byte. It also rejects
    a buffer that wraps around or ends above PHYS_BASE. size 0 is
    always allowed.
  - check_valid_str(s): walks the string one character at a time,
    checking each address before reading it, until it reaches '\0'.

In syscall_handler(), we first check that all 4 bytes of the system
call number at esp are valid. For each system call we check all 4
bytes of each argument on the stack, then check the buffer or string
the arguments point to. Only after everything has passed do we call
the sys_* function, which can then read and write the user memory
directly. For example, the read system call:

    case SYS_READ:
      check_valid_ptr (stack_ptr + 1);
      check_valid_ptr ((char *)(stack_ptr + 1) + 3);
      check_valid_ptr (stack_ptr + 2);
      check_valid_ptr ((char *)(stack_ptr + 2) + 3);
      check_valid_ptr (stack_ptr + 3);
      check_valid_ptr ((char *)(stack_ptr + 3) + 3);
      check_valid_buf ((void *) *(stack_ptr + 2), *(stack_ptr + 3));
      f->eax = sys_read (*(stack_ptr + 1), (void *) *(stack_ptr + 2),
                         *(stack_ptr + 3));
      break;

After the checks, read() writes into the user buffer with file_read()
or input_getc(), and write() reads it with putbuf() (for fd 1, all in
one call) or file_write().

As a safety net, page_fault() in exception.c kills the process with
exit(-1) if the fault came from user code, or if the kernel faulted on
a user address. So even a missed check cannot crash the kernel.

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full page (4,096 bytes) of data
>> to be copied from user space into the kernel.  What is the least
>> and the greatest possible number of inspections of the page table
>> (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that might result?  What about
>> for a system call that only copies 2 bytes of data?  Is there room
>> for improvement in these numbers, and how much?

For a buffer, check_valid_buf() calls pagedir_get_page() for the first
byte, once for the start of each later page the buffer crosses, and
once for the last byte.

  4,096 bytes:
    least    = 2  (the buffer is page-aligned: first byte + last byte,
                   both on the same page)
    greatest = 3  (not aligned: first byte, start of the second page,
                   last byte)

  2 bytes:
    least    = 2  (both bytes on the same page: first byte + last byte,
                   even though it is the same page)
    greatest = 3  (the 2 bytes straddle a page boundary: first byte,
                   start of the second page, and last byte, which is
                   the same address as the second check)

(Checking the system call number and arguments on the stack adds 2
calls per 4-byte value; that is the same for both cases.)

Improvement: we could check each page only once. Then a page-aligned
4,096-byte buffer needs 1 call, an unaligned one needs 2, and a 2-byte
buffer needs 1 (or 2 if it crosses a page boundary). This is the
minimum possible when validating in advance. With the page-fault
method (get_user/put_user), there would be 0 page table lookups; the
MMU checks the access instead.

>> B5: Briefly describe your implementation of the "wait" system call
>> and how it interacts with process termination.

sys_wait() simply calls process_wait(pid). Parent and child meet
through the shared struct child_process and its wait_sema.

PARENT (process_wait):
  1. Search child_process_list for a record with this pid.
     Not found (not a direct child) -> return -1 immediately.
  2. waited already true (second wait) -> return -1 immediately.
  3. Set waited = true.
  4. sema_down(&child->wait_sema). This blocks until the child has
     exited, or returns at once if it already has.
  5. Read exit_status, remove the record from the list, free it.
  6. Return the status.

CHILD (exit and process_exit):
  1. exit(status) stores status in child_process->exit_status, prints
     "name: exit(status)" and calls thread_exit().
     A process killed by the kernel (bad pointer, page fault) goes
     through syscall_exit(-1), so its status is -1. exit_status also
     starts at -1, so a child that fails to load reports -1.
  2. thread_exit() calls process_exit(), which closes all open files
     and the executable.
  3. With interrupts off, it sets exited = true and calls
     sema_up(&wait_sema), which wakes the parent if it is waiting.

Because a semaphore remembers an "up", the parent always calls
sema_down, and it works whether the child exits before or after the
parent calls wait.

>> B6: Any access to user program memory at a user-specified address
>> can fail due to a bad pointer value.  Such accesses must cause the
>> process to be terminated.  System calls are fraught with such
>> accesses, e.g. a "write" system call requires reading the system
>> call number from the user stack, then each of the call's three
>> arguments, then an arbitrary amount of user memory, and any of
>> these can fail at any point.  This poses a design and
>> error-handling problem: how do you best avoid obscuring the primary
>> function of code in a morass of error-handling?  Furthermore, when
>> an error is detected, how do you ensure that all temporarily
>> allocated resources (locks, buffers, etc.) are freed?  In a few
>> paragraphs, describe the strategy or strategies you adopted for
>> managing these issues.  Give an example.

Strategy 1: check everything first, in one place. All pointer checks
happen in syscall_handler() before any sys_* function runs, using three
small helpers (check_valid_ptr, check_valid_buf, check_valid_str). The
helpers never return on failure; they call sys_exit(-1). So the sys_*
functions contain only the real work and no pointer error handling.

Strategy 2: never fail while holding a resource. Because all checks
happen before a sys_* function starts, a bad pointer is found before
we take filesys_lock or malloc anything. So in the normal error path
there is nothing to clean up.

Strategy 3: one cleanup path for every way a process ends. Whether a
process calls exit, is killed by a bad pointer, or faults, it goes
through thread_exit() -> process_exit(). process_exit() closes and
frees every open file, closes the executable, frees child records,
updates its own record for the parent, and destroys the page
directory. As a last safety net, if the process somehow dies while
holding filesys_lock, process_exit() releases it so other processes
are not blocked forever.

Example: write(1, buf, 100) where buf + 50 is unmapped.
  1. syscall_handler checks the 4 bytes at esp (call number) - OK.
  2. It checks the 4 bytes of each of the 3 arguments - OK.
  3. check_valid_buf(buf, 100) checks buf (OK), then each page start
     inside the buffer, which is unmapped, so it calls sys_exit(-1).
  4. sys_exit prints "name: exit(-1)" and calls thread_exit(), which
     runs process_exit() and frees everything.
No lock was taken and putbuf() was never called, so nothing leaks.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?

The parent waits on load_sema, and the child reports through
load_success in the shared struct child_process.

PARENT (process_execute):
  1. malloc a struct child_process with load_sema = 0 and
     load_success = false.
  2. Pass it to the new thread inside struct process_info and call
     thread_create().
  3. Add the record to child_process_list.
  4. sema_down(&child->load_sema) -> blocks until the child has tried
     to load.
  5. If load_success is false, return TID_ERROR (-1); otherwise
     return the child's tid.

CHILD (start_process):
  1. Call load().
  2. Store the result in child_proc->load_success.
  3. sema_up(&load_sema) -> wakes the parent.
  4. If loading failed, set exit_status = -1 and call thread_exit().

The parent cannot get past step 4 until the child reaches step 3, so
exec never returns before the load result is known, however the two
threads are scheduled.

sys_exec() does not hold filesys_lock while waiting, because the
child's load() needs that lock; holding it would deadlock.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?

The shared struct child_process is malloced by P and owned by
whichever of P or C finishes last. The fields that decide this
(exited, parent_alive) are only changed with interrupts turned off, so
P and C can never see them in a half-updated state.

P calls wait(C) before C exits:
  P sets waited = true and blocks on wait_sema. When C exits,
  process_exit() sets exited = true and calls sema_up. P wakes,
  reads exit_status, removes and frees the record.

P calls wait(C) after C exits:
  C already set exited = true and called sema_up, so the semaphore's
  value is 1. P's sema_down returns immediately, P reads exit_status
  and frees the record. The record stayed alive because C does not
  free it while P is alive.

P exits without waiting, before C exits:
  In process_exit(), P goes through its child list. C's record has
  exited == false, so P sets parent_alive = false and does not free
  it. Later, when C exits, it sees parent_alive == false and frees the
  record itself.

P exits without waiting, after C exits:
  C's record has exited == true, so P frees it in process_exit().

In every case the record is freed exactly once. C's struct thread
and page directory are freed by the normal thread exit code, no matter
what P does.

Special cases:
  - C fails to load: exit_status stays -1, and C still goes through
    process_exit(), so wait() returns -1 and the record is cleaned up
    the same way.
  - wait() on a pid that is not a direct child, or a second wait on
    the same child, returns -1 without blocking (the record is not in
    the list, or waited is true).
  - Children are not inherited: if P exits, its children are not given
    to any other process.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?

We validate pointers before using them (the first method in the
project description) because it is the simplest to get right. When a
check fails, we know exactly where we are in the code and that we hold
no locks and no memory, so we can just exit the process. With the
page-fault method, a fault can happen anywhere inside a system call,
possibly while holding filesys_lock or with memory allocated, which
makes cleanup much harder.

The downside is speed: we look up the page table even though the
pointer is almost always valid, and check_valid_str() checks every
character. For this project, correctness mattered more than speed.

>> B10: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?

Advantages:
  - Simple: a linked list per process, so there is no fixed limit on
    open files other than memory.
  - Each process can only reach its own files, because lookups search
    only the current thread's list.
  - Cleanup is easy: process_exit() just walks the list.
  - Because the counter is shared, fd numbers are never reused, so a
    stale fd can never point to a different, newly opened file.

Disadvantages:
  - get_file() is O(n) in the number of open files; an array or hash
    table would be O(1).
  - The shared counter only goes up, so after about 2^31 opens it
    would overflow.
  - fd numbers are not the smallest free number, as they would be in
    Unix.

>> B11: The default tid_t to pid_t mapping is the identity mapping.
>> If you changed it, what advantages are there to your approach?

We did not change it. pid_t is the same as tid_t, which is fine
because each process has exactly one thread. Keeping the identity
mapping avoids an extra table and makes debugging easier, since the pid
printed by a test is the same as the thread's tid.

